{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/404191-weapons-and-armour","id":404191,"title":"Weapons and armour","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-02-13T00:57:25-08:00","id":404191,"title":"Weapons and armour","content":"I'm about to set a campaign during the 30th years war, from 1618 to 1648. But I lack stats on weapons and armour from that period.<br /> \u200bSo do any of you know of any cWoD book that will have such stats? in advance thanks <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/657-skafte","name":"skafte","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar657_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-13T01:58:50-08:00","id":404298,"title":"","content":"Mage's Sorcerer's Crusade line is going to get you the closest to the 30 Years War period, though it's off by a good degree time-wise. But it still has stats for early military firearms and artillery. You could improve the stats of the weapons a bit to represent the improvements in technology, but I'm not sure it's really worth it unless you've got some significant military history buffs in your group.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-13T03:54:38-08:00","id":404355,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>skafte</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/404191-weapons-and-armour#post404191\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm about to set a campaign during the 30th years war, from 1618 to 1648. But I lack stats on weapons and armour from that period.<br /> \u200bSo do any of you know of any cWoD book that will have such stats? in advance thanks <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>There were many examples of early firearms in WtO books.<br /> <br /> Also, when I was preparing (and I'm still adding thing or two) material and gaming aids for my potential English Civil War chronicle, I scoured a lot of sourcebooks to &quot;stat&quot; weapons of that time. I have &quot;stated&quot; close-combat weapons (including some very unusual), all 3 types of early firearms (matchlock, wheellock and flintclock), various types of artillery, petards and armour. PLus I revised an compiled rules on reloading early firearms and cannons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3479,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","user":"von Lyttenburgh","date":". 02-19-2015, 06:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","name":"von Lyttenburgh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3479_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-13T06:06:18-08:00","id":404415,"title":"","content":"Thanks Heavy Arms, those stats might be usefull<br /> <br /> von Lyttenburgh, any chance your work is available somewhere public? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/657-skafte","name":"skafte","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar657_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-13T08:17:28-08:00","id":404483,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/404191-weapons-and-armour?p=404298#post404298\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mage's Sorcerer's Crusade line is going to get you the closest to the 30 Years War period, though it's off by a good degree time-wise. But it still has stats for early military firearms and artillery. You could improve the stats of the weapons a bit to represent the improvements in technology, but I'm not sure it's really worth it unless you've got some significant military history buffs in your group.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> As Heavy Arms and von Lyttenburgh said, <b>Mage Sorcerer's Crusade</b> is probably your best bet. Go to your library find weapons of the period and compare them to what is in <b>Mage Sorcerer's Crusade </b>and <b>7th Sea </b>is another good one to use not just for guns but the technology of that time. You might have to take a guess, I ran a historical WoD game it took some time getting things together. Now I also think if my memory is right and I don't have the book near me: there is a historical weapons list in <b>WOD: Combat</b> but not sure how close it is to your era on any of them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4949-nene-richards","name":"Nene Richards","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4949_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"70"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-13T10:07:15-08:00","id":404651,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>skafte</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/404191-weapons-and-armour?p=404415#post404415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks Heavy Arms, those stats might be usefull<br /> <br /> von Lyttenburgh, any chance your work is available somewhere public? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Nope, only on my comp.<br /> <br /> But I can repost here if you want. In what you are interested most? Everything or something in particular?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3479,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","user":"von Lyttenburgh","date":". 02-15-2015, 07:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","name":"von Lyttenburgh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3479_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-14T07:33:40-08:00","id":405778,"title":"","content":"Here would be fine, then other may also be able to use them <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> As I don't know all that much about weapons of the period to start with, everything you have would be a help. At least I then have some names to go with","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/657-skafte","name":"skafte","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar657_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-15T18:37:27-08:00","id":407214,"title":"","content":"Now, a disclamer of sorts. I've prepared for the game set during the English Civill war, i.e. 1640s. If your game is taking place in some sifferent time or realm (like &quot;The Three Musketeers&quot; during the seige of La Rochelle), they might not be as useful. Also, I advise to make a research about arms, armour and armies of the period.<br /> <br /> Oh, and one more thing - I won't discuss here some &quot;ordinary&quot; weapons that could be found in pretty much any time period (like hatchets, or common knives). I'm gonna focus on some rather characteristic weapons of the time period and try, to the best of my skill, to stat them<br /> <br /> <b>Part One - Melee Weapons.</b><br /> <br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.myarmoury.com/review_aa_haxe.html\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Horseman\u2019s Axe</u></a>: Like the military pick, the horseman\u2019s axe, sometimes confusingly called a <i>Pollaxe</i>, is swiftly falling from use, now only employed by a few cuirassiers and harquebusiers. It consists of a modest axehead mounted on a wooden or metal shaft and also like the military pick, features a rearward-facing pick.<br /> <br /> Min.Str: 3. Damage: S+4L. <hr />- <b><u>Mace</u></b>: Something of an antique oddity in the armies of the Western Europe, the mace is still used in Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth as a weapon, but primarily serves as a badge of rank. <i>Poruczhik </i>(the junior officer serving each <i>Rotmistr</i>) use the <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Maza_de_armas.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Piernacz</a>, an all-metal flanged mace, sometimes decorated with silver. <i>Rotmistrs </i>(captains) use larger, more elaborate flanged maces called <a href=\"http://www.trocadero.com/101antiques/items/1069037/catphoto.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Buzdygan</a>. The four <i>Hetmans </i>(highest-ranked commanders) use <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/Bu%C5%82awa_Rydza-%C5%9Amig%C5%82ego.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">Bulawas</a>, a spherical, pear- or onion-shaped mace, heavily decorated with gilt, silver, and jewels.<br /> <br /> Min.Str: 2. Damage: S+2B/L. Might grant situational bonus to Social rolls against persons recognizing its symbolism.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.imbrium.ru/upload/iblock/a19/DSC_0295.JPG\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Military Pick</u></a>: The combination hammer and pick is rapidly disappearing from the battlefield, now used only by a few harquebusiers and cuirassiers in most of Europe. Only among Polish-Lithuanian and Russian nobles does it see continued extensive use in the form of the <a href=\"http://www.xenophon-mil.org/rushistory/medievalarmor/russ39.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Czekan</a>, an all-steel weapon used more as a symbol of authority and duelling weapon as an implement of battle. The Polish-Lithuanian nobles still prefer swords in battle, however, so it is most often seen off the battlefield.<br /> <br /> Min.Str: 2. Difficlty: 7. Damage: S+2L. Might grant situational bonus to Social rolls against persons recognizing its symbolism. <hr />- <a href=\"http://i873.photobucket.com/albums/ab300/Dragon-Torc/BLADES/DudgeonKnife-DesertIronwoodPatternw.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Dudgeon</a>: So-called because its handle is most frequently made of dudgeon (boxwood), this double-edged dagger is also called a <a href=\"http://www.myarmoury.com/images/features/pic_spot_dirks04_s.gif\" target=\"_blank\">Bollock Knife</a> because of the distinctive shape of its shaft and the two oval swellings that form its guard. It has been in common use in England, Scotland, and the Dutch Republic for several hundred years.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 1. Difficulty: 5. Damage S+1L.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.myarmoury.com/images/features/pic_spot_dirks09.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Dirk</a>. A Scottish Highlander development of the dudgeon, the dirk has a wide blade around 30cm in length, and a short handle carved from dark wood that still resembles that of the dudgeon in most cases. In the Highlander fighting style it is held in the off-hand shield-arm.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 1. Difficulty: 5. Damage S+1L.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.medieval-weaponry.co.uk/acatalog/SH2208-1000.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Main Gauche</a>: Although it originated in France, the main gauche parrying dagger is the off-hand weapon of choice for many fencers throughout Western Europe. A long, double-edged dagger, it has a guard ring and long quillons for superior parrying, either straight, S-shaped, or down-curved. <a href=\"http://media.liveauctiongroup.net/i/8347/9751276_1.jpg?v=8CD1F47030A8C70\" target=\"_blank\">Some feature broad metal guards</a> to protect the hand further and these add 1 Armour to the wielding limb.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 1. Difficulty: 5. Damage S+1L. Parrying difficulty 4.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcR_6jVUxKX8vYafXxfDdBRJIcImJEtBeoOUi5dC5DySlOfyPc8NDA\" target=\"_blank\">Sword Breaker</a>: This broad-bladed, singleedged parrying dagger has long straight quillons and a ring guard. It is immediately recognisable due to the fact the back of the blade is serrated with around a dozen teeth designed to catch and hold the attacking blades. After the succesful performace of the combat maneuvre &quot;disarm&quot;, Sword Breaker's armed opponent roll Str - at three or more successes enemy's bladed weapon is broken.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 1. Difficulty: 5. Damage S+1L.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.therionarms.com/reenact/therionarms_c1157.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Trident Dagger</a>: At first glance a normal main gauche, the trident dagger is cunningly wrought so that with the press of a button, the edges of the blade spring out to form a wide trident-like array ideal for parrying and catching enemy blades. The difficulty of &quot;disarm&quot; combat maneuvre is lowered by 1.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 1. Difficulty: 5. Damage S+1L. <hr />- <a href=\"http://www.museum.ru/imgB.asp?41863\" target=\"_blank\">Bardiche</a>: Used by some troops in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth and being a signature weapon of Russian <i>Streltsy</i>, the bardiche has a long, cleaver-like axehead (typically around 25-90cm long) attached to a stout wooden pole, 1.2-1.5m in length. It is regarded as a brutal weapon, and the Streltsy are known <a href=\"http://bibliotekar.ru/poligon/mush/3.files/image001.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">to use it as a musket rest</a> as well as a weapon.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 3. Difficulty: 7. Damage S+5L. Requires 2 hands.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.ljplus.ru/img4/a/n/antoin/34-Brandistock.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Brandistock</a>: A rare polearm, primarily used in Italy but found elsewhere too, the brandistock consists of a 1.5m long metal tube covered in leather or a wooden veneer, and is used by well-to-do civilians and off-duty officers as a walking stick. It does, however, conceal a more lethal armament, for with a sturdy jerk downwards, three blades emerge and lock in place. The central thrusting blade is usually around 30-45cm and the two side blades are much shorter at around 10-15cm. It is sometimes called a <i>Feather Staff</i>.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 3. Difficulty: 7. Damage S+3L. Requires 2 hands.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.artic.edu/aic/collections/citi/images/standard/WebLarge/WebImg_000247/171428_2912466.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Glaive</a>: The glaive is a wooden shaft 1.8-2.1m in length topped with a singlededged blade, typically 45cm long at the base of which is a tassel. Like halberds, partisans, and pollaxes, it was used as a symbol of authority by sergeants and some officers, as well as by military bodyguards, albeit far more rarely than the halberd or partisan. Those belonging to officers and bodyguards were likely to be engraved or otherwise decorated.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 3. Difficulty: 7. Damage S+5L. Requires 2 hands.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://blogmedieval.com/wp-content/gallery/halberds/xg603.png\" target=\"_blank\">Halberd</a>: Halberdiers are employed within the infantry of Habsburg Germany and Spain, supporting <i>Rondartschiers</i> (bucklermen) and guarding key positions, ensigns, and supply wagons. Outside of the Holy Roman Empire and the guards of various palaces, halberds are generally only used by sergeants as symbols of rank, and by the bodyguards of high-ranking offi cers. Like all halberds, it has a long wooden shaft topped by a steel axehead, thrusting spike, and rearward hook, the base of the head is decorated with a tassel.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 3. Difficulty: 7. Damage S+5L. Requires 2 hands.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.discoverthemiddleages.com/img/full/Spontoon%201.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Half-Pike</a>: The half-pike is found in most European armies, but is much less common than the full-length pike. It is, however, more versatile due to its length of only 2.4-2.7m and sees use on the battlefi eld and in shipboard actions. Irish soldiers make much use of half-pikes with bodkin-style spearheads.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 2. Difficulty: 7. Damage S+3L. Requires 2 hands.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.swordforum.com/forums/attachment.php?attachmentid=4108&amp;d=1035034460\" target=\"_blank\">Jeddart Staff</a>: Originating in Jedburgh, Scotland, this Highland polearm is a 1.8-2.1m long pole mounting a 1.2m long blade. The blade is singled-edged, slim, very slightly curved, and suitable for cutting or thrusting.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 3. Difficulty: 7. Damage S+5L. Requires 2 hands.<br /> <br /> - <u><b>Cavalry Lance</b></u>: Lancers have largely fallen out of favour in most of Europe with the main exception being the cavalry of PLC - <a href=\"http://wordwarriorsandiego.files.wordpress.com/2012/09/husaria.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">the Husaria</a> - and the Tatars. These use a lance called a <i>Kopjo</i>, a hollowed-out 5m wooden lance with a ball-shaped handguard and light silk pennant. Due to its hollowed out nature it is a one-use weapon, nearly always shattering after the first solid strike, at which point it is discarded and the horseman\u2019s swords are brought to bear. In the British Isles, only the Scots currently make any real use of the lance, a high proportion of their few cavalry using a simple spear-like lance 2.4-2.7m in length.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 3. Damage 8L or Str L.<br /> <br /> - <u><b>Officer's Staff or baton</b></u>: A symbol of officer rank, sometimes used instead of a more sturdy polearm such as a partisan or pollaxe, the leading staff is a 1.2-1.5m wooden shaft topped with an intricate spearheadshaped decoration. It is a flimsy device, of little use as an actual weapon.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 2. Difficulty: 7. Damage S +1-3 B. Requires 2 hands. Might grant situational bonus to Social rolls against persons recognizing its symbolism.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.claymore-armoury.co.uk/images/scottish_swords/Large/No4-18th-lochaber.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Lochaber Axe</a>: Another Highlander invention, the Lochaber axe features a 1.5-1.8m shaft and broad, cleaver-like axe blade averaging 45cm length. A sturdy backward-facing hook (cleek) is attached to the top of the shaft or back of the blade, useful for hooking opponents, especially cavalry of whom the Highlanders have few. The butt end of the shaft features a crude metal thrusting tip, increasing the weapon\u2019s versatility.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 3. Difficulty: 7. Damage S+5L. Requires 2 hands.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.dpm.org.cn/www_oldweb/English/E/e53/images/wenwu/20700.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">Partisan</a>: The Partisan is issued to infantry officers and the bodyguards of commanders throughout Europe as a symbol of rank, though few use it as a weapon, preferring to use their swords and pistols instead. It features a sturdy wooden shaft, typically around 1.8m in length, topped with a long, broad, tapering spearhead with two curved flukes at its base. Tassels adorn the base of the spearhead and it is sometimes engraved and otherwise artfully crafted.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 2. Difficulty: 7. Damage S+4L. Requires 2 hands.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.cardiffrose.com/pike.gif\" target=\"_blank\">Pike</a>: The ruling close-combat infantry weapon, the pike is generally around 5m in length and can be found heavily employed by nearly every army in Europe. Although pikemen were equal in numbers to musketeers only a handful of years ago, the increasing use and effectiveness of muskets means that numbers of pikemen are falling to as few as 20%-30% of the infantry now fielded.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 3. Difficulty: 7. Damage S+4L. Requires 2 hands.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://armor.com/images/pole010a_s.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Pollaxe</a>: Rarely used by infantry officers instead of a partisan or leading staff, the pollaxe has a sturdy wooden shaft, 1.2-2.1m in length topped with a hammerhead, backward-facing pick, and thrusting spike.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 3. Difficulty: 7. Damage S+5L. Requires 2 hands.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/Mousceteor.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Swinefeather</a>: Until recently extensively used by the forces of the late Gustav II Adolf, King of Sweden, the swinefeather is a wooden stake, 1.5-1.8m long, with a spearhead at the top and iron spike at the base. Also called a <i>Swedish Feather</i>, it is used by some musketeers in Western Europe as a guarding device, thrust into the ground in front of the musketeer and angled forward so as to be set against any cavalry charges that might come.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 2. Difficulty: 7. Damage S+3L. Requires 2 hands. <hr />- <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/Schiavona-Morges.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Basket-Hilted Broadsword</a>: The basket-hilted broadsword is the standard military sword of cavalry in nations throughout Europe. All such swords feature straight cut-and-thrust blades, nearly always double-edged though some singled-edged backswords existed. In England, the <a href=\"http://www.medieval-weaponry.co.uk/acatalog/SH2076-920-1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Mortuary Sword</a> sports an intricate half-basket hilt, while in the Dutch Republic, the <a href=\"http://www.vikingsword.com/vb/attachment.php?attachmentid=102603&amp;stc=1\" target=\"_blank\">Walloon Sword</a> is favoured, with two large shell-guards (often decoratively pierced) and a knucklebow. The elegant Italian <a href=\"http://www.armorymarek.com/public/default/userfiles/image/schiavona_basketsword/m-016.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Schiavona</a> has a cat\u2019s-head pommel and cage-like hilt whereas the Polish <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/Claymore-Morges.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Palasz</a> usually has a simpler basket-hilt or knucklebow. Scottish Highlanders able to afford one use the basket-hilted <a href=\"http://www.cindyvallar.com/Swords2.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Claidheamh M\u00f2r</a> (Claymore) as their primary weapon of choice, usually in conjunction with a <i>targe</i> (buckler) shield strapped to their other arm and dirk held in the shield hand.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 2. Damage S+3/4L (one/two hands).<br /> <br /> - <u><b>Great Sword</b></u>: With the days of the <a href=\"http://vignette1.wikia.nocookie.net/warrior/images/7/73/%D0%9B%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B4%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%85%D1%82_%D1%81_%D1%84%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B1%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%BC.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20121117125017&amp;path-prefix=ru\" target=\"_blank\">Landsknechts</a> long past, the great sword has all but vanished from use in Europe. The only notable exception is the Scottish <a href=\"http://www.wildhighlanders.com/images/wild_highlanders/scot.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Claidheamh d\u00e0 L\u00e0imh</a>, sometimes confusingly called a \u201cClaymore\u201d, a straightbladed, double-edged great sword averaging 1.4m in length, with a long downswept cross hilt. It is far less common among Highlanders than the basket-hilted <i>Claidheamh M\u00f2r</i>.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 4. Damage S+5/6L. (one/two hands)<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/MuseeMarine-sabre-p1000456.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Hanger</a>: The Hanger is a hunting sword, a short blade (typically 45-60cm), nearly always single-edged, sometimes slightly curved, with a knucklebow to protect the hand. Frequently well made, and often decorated by wealthier owners, it is used to kill injured or otherwise immobilised prey but also makes a handy defensive weapon. It is a popular weapon throughout Europe, and is coming to be used frequently aboard ship, where it will eventually lead to the later <i>Cutlass</i>.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 2. Damage S+2L.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Koncerz.png\" target=\"_blank\">Koncerz</a>: This unusual sword is used exclusively by the famed Polish Husaria, and features an unedged, slender blade around 1.4m in length, designed to thrust only and effectively pierce mail armour. The grip is protected by a simple but sturdy knucklebow.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 2. Damage S+3L.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.medieval-weaponry.co.uk/acatalog/SH2206N-1000.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Rapier</a>: The civilian weapon of the gentleman, the rapier is commonplace among those able to afford it, and can be found throughout Western Europe. Many rapiers feature elaborate swept hilts, but cage hilts, <a href=\"http://www.darkwoodarmory.com/images/ShellPapp1.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">Pappenheimer hilts</a> (elegant combinations of swept hilt and pierced shell guards), and cup hilt rapier styles are also popular, the latter especially in Spain.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 1. Damage S+2L.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://globe-views.com/dcim/dreams/sabre/sabre-03.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Sabre</a>: The <a href=\"http://salon.mieczy.pl/images/szabla1976_3.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Szabla</a>, the sabre beloved of Polish-Lithuanian cavalry has a curved single-edged blade and knucklebow. Scottish Highlanders also use a rare sabrelike sword, the <a href=\"http://pic20.picturetrail.com/VOL22/550150/809346/26477598.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Turcael</a>, though this more resembles a basket-hilted Claidheamh M\u00f2r broadsword, albeit singled-edged and curved.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 3. Damage S+3L.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.antik-buchwald.de/index.php/exponate/image?format=raw&amp;type=img&amp;id=1479\" target=\"_blank\">- Schweizers\u00e4bel</a>: A rare weapon originating in the Swiss Confederacy, the schweizers\u00e4bel (Swiss Sabre) has a slightly curved, singled-edged blade around 1m in length, and two-handed grip protected by a swept-style hilt. Like many longswords of the past, it can be wielded in one hand or two, and despite its name it is not a cavalry weapon. Its hilt adds 1 Armour to one or both limbs being used to wield it.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 3 (4 if in one hand). Damage S+4/5L (one/two hands).<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/Spada_da_Lato.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Side-Sword</a>: Ubiquitous on the battlefields of Europe as the standard sword of many infantrymen, the side-sword is a straightbladed, double-edged cut-and-thrust sword with S-shaped or straight quillons and often a simple knucklebow.<br /> <br /> Min. Str. 2. Damage S+2L.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3479,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","user":"von Lyttenburgh","date":". 02-18-2015, 08:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","name":"von Lyttenburgh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3479_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-17T12:42:29-08:00","id":409224,"title":"","content":"<b>Part Two - Ranged Weapons.</b><br /> <br /> But first, a little intro about various types of the XVII century firearms:<br /> <br /> - <u><b>Matchlock</b></u> muskets are by far the most common muskets throughout Europe, and feature robust, simple mechanisms that are relatively cheap to produce. Unfortunately, the matchcord (a length of flax) is a serious downside to the weapon. For the weapon to fire, the slow-burning matchcord must have been lit, and so many musketeers spend a great deal of time, effort, and attention keeping their matchcord burning. At least one or two musketeers in a given unit always maintain a burning matchcord to rapidly light their fellows\u2019 when necessity dictates it. This means the amount of matchcord needed can be a burden in terms of supply and weight; it is not unusual for a musketeer to burn through <i>several metres</i> <i>a day</i>, in battle or out. Furthermore, matchcord is notoriously susceptible to the weather and will not light if damp or wet, so keeping it dry is also a constant concern and effort. Even strong wind has an impact, making it more difficult to light and maintain. Finally, the ember glow of lit matchcord is highly visible in darkness or at night, and has ruined many ambushes.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2KTS8PQ06Qo\" target=\"_blank\">This is a video of a mathclock musket fired and reloaded</a>. Yes, it's slow.<br /> <br /> - Compared to the matchlock, the <u><b>wheellock</b></u> is far less problematic; it will spark regardless of damp and does not require matchcord supplies. Its downside is it is a very complex and expensive mechanism to craft and requires frequent maintenance. Despite this, it is the most common mechanism in carbines and pistols at that time period.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wEoecTbp2XM\" target=\"_blank\">Here is a video of a a wheellock carabine fired</a>. Not much better, really.<br /> <br /> - The <u><b>flintlock</b></u> mechanism has all of the benefits of the wheellock, but is also a simpler mechanism to produce, making it ultimately cheaper and easier to manufacture and far less prone to malfunction or serious upkeep. But even at the time of the English Civil War (or in later period of the 30 years War), flintlocks are still relatively new and thus for the moment artificially expensive (raising Resources requirements by 1), though this fact will change rapidly very soon. The most common flintlock mechanisms in England and the Dutch Republic at this moment are the so-called <a href=\"http://www.middlesexvillagetrading.com/images/PDOG_right.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">English Lock</a> and the <a href=\"http://i684.photobucket.com/albums/vv209/Bham_Greaser/Firelocks/MVTCo_English_Lock_Doglock_Pistol-a.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Doglock</a>, a flintlock featuring an external safety catch preventing the mechanism going off half-cocked.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=stSzZ3miwI8\" target=\"_blank\">A Video of a flintlock musket firing</a>. Plus - <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SJMbxZ1k9NQ\" target=\"_blank\">a demonstartion of its higher rate of fire</a>.<br /> <br /> Rules-wise this means the following:<br /> <br /> A) <b>Reloading process</b>. Muskets of the time required extremely long time to recharge. The shooter requires about 5 turns to clean the barrel, insert the matchcord, load and prepare to fire a musket. At this time, he should stay in one place and can not take any other action during the process of (unless he wants to start from the beginning). Bad conditions (during the heat of battle, its raining, or in dark) can <i>double</i> or even <i>triple</i> the time it takes to reload.<br /> <br /> B) <b>Wet gunpowder</b>. Character who's gunpowder gets wet, or if he shoots in the rain or in the dense fog can completely ruin his gunpowder if he doesn't take special precautions.<br /> <br /> C) <b>Match cords</b>. Lighting a match cord takes one turn. Its replacement takes one turn also.<br /> <br /> D) <b>Wheellocks</b>. Wheellocks require one turn to tension the mainspring ready for firing, using a special spanner to engage it. It could be done before the battle.<br /> <br /> And now, examples of this time period's <b><u>firearms</u></b>:<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://3.bp.blogspot.com/-AItHJu065nU/TmujrAkJxZI/AAAAAAAAACo/MV3aOujH9O8/s1600/100_0813.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">Donderbus</a>: The \u201cThundergun\u201d was invented in the German states, but was quickly adopted by gunsmiths in the Dutch Republic where it became far better known and appreciated. Available in matchlock and flintlock versions, this short, brass-barrelled musket has a distinctive flared muzzle, and fires 12-15 musket balls in a single shot. This sprays a target at close range, making it a devastating weapon, and the flared barrel makes it easier to load. A few harquebusiers carry donderbuses instead of carbines but it remains a very rare battlefield weapon. Although it can fire assorted scrap in an emergency (reducing the effective range to just 10 metres), doing so can quickly and easily shred or otherwise damage the barrel. In the future, this weapon\u2019s name will be corrupted, becoming <i>Blunderbus</i>.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 7. <u>Damage</u>: 8. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/4. <u>Min. Str</u>: 2. <u>Range</u>: 15 metres.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://nic.sarahparsons.co.uk/images/arquebus/Viewport_Wip08.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Matchlock Arquebus</a>: A weapon now almost completely abandoned by European armies in favour of the Musket, the arquebus is still used by some irregular forces in mainland Europe, such as the Hungarian <a href=\"http://www.hrvatski-vojnik.hr/hrvatski-vojnik/fotogallery/povjesne_odore/big_pictures/Br.%2029/Hajduk.jpg\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Hajduks</i></a>. Lightweight, simple, and short compared to a matchlock musket, it doesn\u2019t require a rest but is comparatively low-powered and lacking<br /> in range.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 8. <u>Damage</u>: 8. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/6. <u>Min. Str</u>: 3. <u>Range</u>: 20 metres.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.militaryheritage.com/images/matchlock1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Matchlock Musket</a>: The standard weapon of the musketeer throughout Europe, the matchlock musket dominates the battlefield despite its relative shortcomings. Although many models are cumbersome and have overlong barrels requiring rests to fire accurately, recent matchlock muskets are lighter and shorter, removing any necessity for a rest to be carried.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 7. <u>Damage</u>: 6. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/5. <u>Min. Str</u>: 3. <u>Range</u>: 35 metres.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://weaponscollector.com/images/111-0073/111-0073a_small.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Matchlock Carbine</a>: Far less common than the wheellock carbine, the matchlock carbine is commonly found in use with Royalist dragoons.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 7. <u>Damage</u>: 5. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/5. <u>Min. Str</u>: 2. <u>Range</u>: 32 metres.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.neahkahnieflintlocks.com/uploads/2/2/8/5/22859252/8675127_orig.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Matchlock Pistol</a>: The matchlock pistol is exceptionally rare simply due to the fact that it was never very popular anyway, and has all but completely been replaced by wheellock and flintlock pistols.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 7. <u>Damage</u>: 5. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/5. <u>Min. Str</u>: 2. <u>Range</u>: 30 metres. Can be concealed under a cloack. <hr />- <a href=\"http://www.guns.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/12/mayflowergun13.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Wheellock Musket</a>: Although more common than the fl intlock musket, the wheellock musket is still a distant second to the matchlock musket in terms of widespread use. It is most commonly issued to dragoons, especially on the Parliamentary side, mounted infantry who would otherwise find a matchlock musket impossible to deal with in the saddle. Unfortunately, Royalist dragoons have had incredible difficulty procuring wheellock muskets, and most have to make do with matchlock carbines instead.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 6. <u>Damage</u>: 6. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/4. <u>Min. Str</u>: 3. <u>Range</u>: 35 metres.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.higgins-collection.org/object_photos/artifact_image/image/12360/2004.01.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Wheellock Carbine</a>: The wheellock carbine is the standard musket of harquebusier cavalry throughout Europe, and has an average barrel length of 76cm.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 6. <u>Damage</u>: 5. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/4. <u>Min. Str</u>: 2. <u>Range</u>: 32 metres.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://media.liveauctiongroup.net/i/8690/9965311_1.jpg?v=8CD4E3CC76E3200\" target=\"_blank\">Wheellock Pistol</a>: Carried in pairs by most cuirassiers and harquebusiers, the wheellock pistol is by far the most common pistol on the battlefield and in use throughout Europe.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 6. <u>Damage</u>: 5. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/4. <u>Min. Str</u>: 2. <u>Range</u>: 30 metres. Can be concealed under a cloack.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"https://s-media-cache-ak0.pinimg.com/736x/2f/c9/f7/2fc9f7b97270a2a837dd4ebd884a099c.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Wheellock Dueling Pistol</a>: The wheellock dueling pistol is identical to the flintlock dueling pistol in every regard save mechanism and being more frequently encountered.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 6. <u>Damage</u>: 5. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/4. <u>Min. Str</u>: 2. <u>Range</u>: 35 metres. Can be concealed under a cloack. <hr />- <a href=\"http://www.militaryheritage.com/images/musket1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Flintlock Musket</a>: Seldom encountered outside of a few Royalist units, flintlock muskets are most often used by artillery guards because the mechanism is much safer to bear near quantities of blackpowder than matchlocks are.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 6. <u>Damage</u>: 7. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/4. <u>Min. Str</u>: 3. <u>Range</u>: 35 metres.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://milpas.cc/rifles/ZFiles/Black%20Powder%20Muskets/Locks/flint01.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Flintlock Carbine</a>: Flintlock carbines are more common among Parliamentary cavalry than Royalist ones, but even there are scarce compared to the more commonplace wheellock carbine.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 6. <u>Damage</u>: 6. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/4. <u>Min. Str</u>: 2. <u>Range</u>: 32 metres.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.royalarmouriesshop.org/images/detailed/1/Flintlock_Pistol_XVII_Century_Replica_312.01.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Flintlock Pistol</a>: Like its musket counterpart, the flintlock pistol is not altogether common yet, but is likely to eclipse the wheellock pistol in use within a few years.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 6. <u>Damage</u>: 5. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/4. <u>Min. Str</u>: 2. <u>Range</u>: 30 metres. Can be concealed under a cloack.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.votesprout.com/replica-images/replica-weapons-45-125.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Flintlock Duellng Pistol</a>: Very much a weapon of the nobility, the dueling pistol is finely crafted and balanced, with a long barrel and appropriate decorative embellishment.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 6. <u>Damage</u>: 5. <u>Rate of fire</u>: 1/4. <u>Min. Str</u>: 2. <u>Range</u>: 35 metres. Can be concealed under a cloack. <hr /><br /> <br /> <u><b>Bows</b></u>.<br /> <br /> Despite the fact, that the battlefield of the XVII belong to various firearms, a few militias, non-European armies and irregular combatants still bear bows.<br /> <br /> - <u><b>Long Bow</b></u>: Yes, Enlgish Long bow is still used in this age. In fact, king Charles I suported such &quot;excellent idea&quot; as so-called &quot;<a href=\"https://leatherworkingreverendsmusings.files.wordpress.com/2012/11/sloapt.gif\" target=\"_blank\">double-armed soildiers</a>&quot;. Despite its antiquity, the yew long bow still sees some use in battle, primarily among some Royalist troops and Highlander mercenaries.<br /> <br /> <u>Damage</u>: Str +4L. <u>Min. Str</u>: 4. <u>Range</u>: 120 metres.<br /> <br /> - <u><b>Recurve Bow</b></u>: The recurve bow is almost only ever found in the hands of Eastern European irregular troops such as <a href=\"https://s-media-cache-ak0.pinimg.com/236x/84/db/72/84db72ae35300af2d6dc61674ec8a921.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">mounted Tatars</a> and Croats.<br /> <br /> <u>Damage</u>: Str +2L. <u>Min. Str</u>: 2. <u>Range</u>: 60 metres.<br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b>Ammunition, Accessories, Modifications and Miscellanea.</b></u><br /> <br /> - <a href=\"https://s-media-cache-ak0.pinimg.com/236x/cb/c9/c1/cbc9c1f43485c4f9439159405ce08b2f.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Grenado</a>: Very rare weapons, grenados are hollow iron spheres big enough to sit comfortably in the hand, filled with gunpowder and fitted with a short piece of a slowly burning matchcord. In decades to come they will be renamed <i>Grenades</i>.<br /> <br /> Storyteller makes secret roll of 1d10. The result is number of combat turns before the explosion.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: Range till target minus Strength. <u>Damage</u>: 8. <u>Area</u>: 1 metre. <u>Range</u>: 10 metres. Can be concealed under a cloack.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/lookandlearn-preview/M/M815/M815979.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Petard</a>. A Petard is essentially an explosive charge that can be attached to a target (often locked doors etc.) and then exploded. A petard consists of a wooden backplate, a metal container with a hole for a fuse and a few pounds of gunpowder. The petardier\u2019s assistant is expected to run through the enemy\u2019s fire (which is often horrendous) and attach the thing (by screwing in a hook to the surface to be blown, then suspending the petard), light a fuse (with a slow match lit at both ends), and, <i>if still alive</i>, retire to a safe distance. The petardier himself is not<br /> expected to make the dash; as a gunpowder expert, he is far too valuable. Any would-be petard setter should note that it is better to run back to one\u2019s own lines in a zig-zag to avoid the path of the metal canister, which tends to rocket backwards as the gunpowder (hopefully) takes its toll in the opposite direction.<br /> <br /> Storyteller makes secret roll of 1d10+3. The result is number of combat turns before the explosion.<br /> <br /> <u>Damage</u>: 10-12 (epicentre), 6-8 (area). <u>Area</u>: 5 metres. <hr /><br /> - <a href=\"http://www.engerisser.de/Bilder/Waffen/Bandelier.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Ammunition Bandoliers</a>: Musketeers and dragoons alike are almost invariably equipped with ammunition bandoliers, leather baldrics from which small wooden, pottery, or tin containers are suspended used for containing pre-measured charges of blackpowder. 8-15 containers may be found on a bandolier, but the most common number is 12, nicknamed the \u201cTwelve Apostles\u201d.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://thumbs.dreamstime.com/x/gunpowder-flask-7413903.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Gunpowder Flask</a>: A treated leather bag or flask containing enough gunpowder for 12 shots.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://fc08.deviantart.net/fs50/i/2009/327/a/8/Shot_pouch_and_pick_by_Seamus612.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Shot pouch</a>: A cloth or leather pouch of 24 lead balls suitable for a musket or pistol.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.therifleshoppe.com/images/trs_pics/933/Matchlock_Cord.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">Matchcord</a>: Slow-burning matchcord, sold by the metre. Someone maintaining a burning matchcord consumes half a metre for every hour of appropriate readiness, or 4m per day on average in battle or on guard duty. <hr />- <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/P%C3%B3%C5%82hak.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Combination Melee Weapon and Pistol</a>: Since viable alternatives to matchlocks began to surface, inventive craftsmen have combined the mechanisms of flintlock and wheellock pistols with various melee weapons. Grossly inaccurate and costly, this has not prevented such combination weapons from becoming popular with well-heeled individuals, in particular <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Elgin_cutlass_pistol.jpg/1280px-Elgin_cutlass_pistol.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">the combination of wheellock or flintlock pistol and hanger sword</a> for use when hunting. Due to the imbalance inherent in such a combination weapon, use of the pistol suffers a +1 Difficulty<br /> <br /> - <u><b>Rifling</b></u>: Some muskets and pistols are rifled, but these are rare, expensive weapons, not favoured in warfare due to how readily blackpowder fouls a rifled barrel if it is not meticulously and frequently cleaned. Of the few rifled arms available, the most common is the rifled wheellock musket, though rifled flintlock muskets are also being produced. These rifles are commonly called <i>Fowling Pieces</i>, and are primarily used by hunters and gamekeepers. Such individuals are sometimes employed by Parliamentarian and Royalist forces as marksmen in siege situations, tasked with picking off officers and artillery crew at range. Rifling cannot be applied to a donderbus.<br /> <br /> <u>Range </u>is increased by 5 metres.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3479,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","user":"von Lyttenburgh","date":". 02-22-2015, 01:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","name":"von Lyttenburgh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3479_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-18T16:27:52-08:00","id":410693,"title":"","content":"<b>Part 3 - Armour.</b><br /> <br /> In 1632 saw the light &quot;<i>Militaire Instructions for the Cav'llrie</i>&quot; by one John Cruso. Portrayed here <a href=\"http://www.nam.ac.uk/microsites/war-horse/wp-content/uploads/2011/10/32441.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">full-plate armour wearing &quot;dragoons</a>&quot; have as much connection with reality of the XVII century as the army of battle eunuchs. Even cuirassiers wore much lighter multi-piece armour, often without several elements. By that time full plate armour was pretty much good only for some official functions, parades and <a href=\"http://www.albion-prints.com/ekmps/shops/albionprint/images/houbraken-c1750-folio-antique-portrait.-henry-ireton-deupty-of-ireland-%5B2%5D-92717-p.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">posing for portraits</a>.<br /> <br /> Still, the improvements in firearms slowly but surely are making any kind of heavy armour absolet. All types of armour of this time period gain -5 to their Protection against firearms.<br /> <br /> Existing at this period armour can be easily broken down in to two types: <i>leather</i> and <i>plate</i>. <hr /><br /> -<a href=\"http://cdn.epiphanydigest.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/06/antique-cuirass-breastplate.jpg?d7b92f\" target=\"_blank\"> Breastplate &amp; Backplate</a>: Also called a <i>Cuirass</i> or <i>Corslet</i>, this armour is worn by harquebusiers, cuirassiers, husaria, pikemen, halberdiers, bucklermen and siege engineers. Often sold as being bullet proof, though this is seldom the case, and many supposed test bullet dents are actually made with the end of a ball-tipped hammer. <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Polish_Hussar_half-armour_Winged_Riders.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Husaria</a> have their breastplates polished and decorated with brass fittings. Many cavalrymen, especially cuirassiers, and nearly all infantrymen of the types mentioned above, have breastplates with tassets attached (see below), though the tassets are discarded in the case of most infantrymen except siege engineers.<br /> <br /> <u>Protection</u>: 5B/6L. <u>Soak Difficulty</u>: Bashing 4, Lethal 5. <u>Penalty</u>: -2. <u>Min.Str</u>: 3.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.allenantiques.com/images/mini-A-181.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Tassets</a>: Two types of tassets are currently worn: <i>Short Tassets</i> and <i>Long Tassets</i>. Short tassets are essentially <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4a/Faulds.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">plate skirts attached to the front of many breastplates</a> with hinges to protect the abdomen and groin. Long tassets are worn by cuirassiers and attach the same way, but are articulated pieces that extend from the waist down the front of each leg to the knee where they are secured, giving protection to both the abdomen and both legs. Neither type can be worn without a supporting breastplate.<br /> <br /> <u>Protection</u>: 5B/6L. <u>Soak Difficulty</u>: Bashing 4, Lethal 5. <u>Penalty</u>: -2. <u>Min.Str</u>: 3.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.allenantiques.com/images/A-163.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Vambrace</a>: Now only worn by cuirassiers, a vambrace is an articulated plate sleeve formed of a combination of pauldron, rerebrace, couter, and vambrace.<br /> <br /> <u>Protection</u>: 4B/5L. <u>Soak Difficulty</u>: Bashing 4, Lethal 5. <u>Penalty</u>: -1. <u>Min.Str</u>: 2.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.spandaumilitariashop.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/06/tn_DSCF80401.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Bridle Gauntlet</a>: An articulated cavalry gauntlet that covers the hand and entire forearm up to the elbow, the bridle gauntlet is worn on the left arm of a few harquebusiers, leaving the right hand unimpeded for easier pistol use. Polish husaria wear a pair of similar gauntlets on both arms called <i>Karvash</i>.<br /> <br /> <u>Protection</u>: 4B/4L. <u>Soak Difficulty</u>: Bashing 4, Lethal 5. <u>Penalty</u>: -1. <u>Min.Str</u>: 2. <hr />- <a href=\"http://jmaucoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/112886.jpg?w=453\" target=\"_blank\">Buff Coat</a>: A shortened version of <i>Buffalo</i> despite being made from cowhide, the buff coat is a treated leather coat, skirted and often sleeved too. It is durable and provides some protection, but most importantly provides freedom of movement. All harquebusiers are equipped with sleeved buff coats, while a rare few infantry officers and musketeer units have <a href=\"https://farm5.staticflickr.com/4008/4366891884_c6df2d881e.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">sleeveless buff coats</a>, principally the Trained Bands of London. The biggest drawback of the buff coat is the fact that it quickly soaks up water and rain and can take days to dry out.<br /> <br /> <u>Protection</u>: 1B/2L. <u>Soak Difficulty</u>: Lethal 7. <u>Penalty</u>: -1. <u>Min.Str</u>: 1<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.thetimeseller.com/images/product/1/small/ps_1_1_6724.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Thigh-Length Cavalry Boots</a>: The thick thigh-length boots worn by many cavalry, especially harquebusiers, and some dragoons, provide some protection to the legs but are commonly turned down outside of combat.<br /> <br /> <u>Protection</u>: 0B/1L. <u>Soak Difficulty</u>: Lethal 7. <u>Penalty</u>: No. <u>Min.Str</u>: 1 <hr />- <u><b>Helmet</b></u>: Helmets of many types can be found among the soldiery of Europe. Harquebusiers, Polish husaria, siege engineers, and some cuirassiers make use of <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Dutch_-_Zischagge_Helmet_-_Walters_511366_-_Three_Quarter.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Capeline helmets</a>, frequently called <i>Lobster Pots</i>, but called <i>Zisch\u00e4gge</i> in German and <a href=\"http://www.donjon.ru/published/publicdata/DONJONADBDONJON/attachments/SC/products_pictures/2pw_enl2r_enl.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Szyszak</a> in Polish and Russian. This is a pot helmet with a fixed peak, cheek guards, articulated aventail, and nasal bar or tribar visor (the latter especially common in England). Cuirassiers also make use of <a href=\"https://s-media-cache-ak0.pinimg.com/originals/de/5c/64/de5c640676b5aee5e7bce0e0b75541d7.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Close Helms</a>, knightly helms that fully enclose the head and featured hinged visors. Pikemen, halberdiers, bucklermen and the musketeers of the Trained London Bands are most commonly equipped with <a href=\"http://www.thevikingstore.co.uk/ekmps/shops/thevikingstore1/images/larp-helmet-alberto-morion-helmet-%5B2%5D-2892-p.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Morion helmets</a>, a style of helmet widely used by the Spanish conquistadors and distinguished by its front and rear pointed peaks and reinforcing top comb. Other helmets frequently used by Pikemen include the <a href=\"http://images.denhams.com/485/485lot349.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Cabasett</a>, a tall-crowned helmet with a narrow brim, <a href=\"http://fartovy.ru/images/kupit_rycarskii_shlem_3_6.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Birnhelm</a>, almost identical to the morion but with a smaller comb and narrower brim, and <a href=\"http://www.allenantiques.com/images/burgonet2.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Burgonet</a>, a domed helmet with fitted peak, tail, reinforcing crest, and cheek guards. Some cuirassiers also make use of the burgonet, usually with a falling buffe visor. The rarest, and certainly most distinctive, helmet is the <a href=\"http://www.thornber.net/cheshire/imagefiles/01atoc/congleton07.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Metal Hat</a>, literally a wide-brimmed civilian hat, but crafted from metal plate and fitted with a sliding nasal guard. This strange helmet is exclusively the domain of the noble and wealthy, as can be testified by the fact that the late King Charles had one.<br /> <br /> <i>Open Helmets</i>: <u>Protection</u>: 2B/3L. <u>Penalty</u>: -1. <u>Min.Str</u>: 2<br /> <i>Close Helmet</i>s: <u>Protection</u>: 3B/4L. <u>Penalty</u>: -2. <u>Min.Str</u>: 2<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/Secrete_(helmet)_lincoln_transparent.png\" target=\"_blank\">Secrete</a>: The only dedicated type of armour encountered in use among the otherwise unarmoured musketeers and dragoons, the secrete is still uncommon, a metal skullcap that can be easily worn unnoticed beneath a hat or cap. It provides little serious protection as it is so form-fitting, light, and only covers the top of the head, but is favoured by those soldiers and officers who want to remain unencumbered, or simply prefer the appeal of wearing a fashionable hat rather than a helmet.<br /> <br /> <u>Protection</u>: 1B/1L. <u>Penalty</u>: No. <u>Min.Str</u>: 1. <hr />- <a href=\"http://www.medieval-weaponry.co.uk/acatalog/AB0127-1000.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Buckler</a>: Also called a <a href=\"http://www.tharkis.com/images/targe/targe60.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Targe</a> or <a href=\"http://www.armae.com/Photos/Boucliers/rondache_MS162C.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Rondache</a>, the Buckler is a small shield, handheld or bound to the forearm. Its main use is among the Scottish Highlanders who use a studded, wooden buckler in combination with a dirk and basket-hilted <i>Claidheamh M\u00f2r</i> broadsword. Bucklermen saw battlefield use until recent years in the armies of Spain as front rank infantry, though they were something of a failed experiment. They were armoured with helmet and breastplate, but armed with a steel buckler, side-sword, and sometimes a matchlock pistol or two.<br /> <br /> Parry difficulty: 4.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3479,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","user":"von Lyttenburgh","date":". 02-18-2015, 11:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","name":"von Lyttenburgh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3479_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-19T09:33:59-08:00","id":411682,"title":"","content":"This is wonderful! Thank you, von Lyttenburgh!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />Avatar commissioned from the incomparable Melissa Uran.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/966-redford_blade","name":"Redford_Blade","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar966_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"90"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-19T18:18:07-08:00","id":412281,"title":"","content":"<br /> <u><b>Part 4 - Artillery.</b></u><br /> <br /> Relatively rare, very expensive and usually very inaccurate, artillery is often more useful due to the fear it produces than its actual effectiveness on the field. Pieces are made individually, to order, and armies often employ a motley assortment of newly commissioned artillery alongside pieces that have been in use for generations. Shifting artillery is very difficult \u2013 the sheer weight and size of most artillery means that it has to be transported using limbers (wooden shafts that can be attached to a team of horses). Even thus limbered, the roads of that time are such that transportation takes a long time \u2013 the artillery train often takes several days, or even weeks, to catch up with an army on the move.<br /> <br /> All artillery weapons use the Artillery skill of whoever is in command of the gun when being fired. Botches on an Artillery roll will cause the weapon to be put out of action until repaired (which will take days), or represent a crack to the barrel that renders the gun irreparable, or, if the Storyteller is feeling particularly cruel, cause everybody in the immediate vicinity to be blown to pieces.<br /> <br /> Particular <u>rules</u> apply to all artillery pieces:<br /> <br /> A) <u>Damage</u>. Damage from guns affect everyone in a radius of 3 meters from the cannonbal's blast. All within a radius of 6 meters receive half damage (this rule does not apply to light artillery).<br /> <br /> B) <u>Canister</u>. Almost every weapon can fire canister or grapeshot (difficulty 7, range 100 meters). Each success on Artillery throw equals to 6 <u>levels</u> of damage to every living (and unnliving) being in a cone with a diameter of 100 m. Opponents standing at a distance of 100-200 meters get 3 <u>levels</u> of damage.<br /> <br /> C) <u>Transportation</u>. First number indicates a number of horses or bulls required to move the artillery piece, the second - a number of men if beast of burden are not available.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> -<a href=\"http://2.bp.blogspot.com/-SX2XDRcJJmg/UgQUM4Q9XYI/AAAAAAAAUCo/wXM3HpHlBMY/s1600/StPArtMus2013-208.JPG\" target=\"_blank\"> Light Artillery</a>: Lighter artillery pieces, including the famous &quot;leather cannons&quot; of Gustavus II Adolf of Sweden. Scottish armies, for example, often use light cannons as heavier weapons are almost impossible to mpve through rough mountain territory. Such weapons might be carried on pack animals before assembly.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 7. <u>Damage</u>: 10. <u>Reload time</u>: 5 mins. <u>Range</u>: 500 metres. <u>Transpportation</u>: 2/8. <u>Crew</u>: 2.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"https://shard3.1stdibs.us.com/archivesE/upload/9814/23_13/org_1990b/1990b_l.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Cannons</a> and <a href=\"http://corkheritage.ie/wp-content/uploads/2009/06/cannon-b-300x225.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Siege Cannons</a>: Siege guns and other large cannons strike fear into the heart of enemy troops. However, they are very heavy, loading time is lengthy, and they are extremely inaccurate and require a lot of maintenance. A large team of horses (4-16 depending on the exact size of the cannon) are required to move them at all and once set up they cannot easily be shifted. Being deployed in a static position beforehand, they are therefore of limited use in battle. When used in siege warfare, their slow rate of fire, tendency to break, and the scarcity of suitable ammunition (although some can fire heavy stones in a pinch) mean that they are used as much to wear down defenders\u2019 morale as to cause casualties or a serious breach in the defences.<br /> <br /> - <i>Cannon</i>: <u>Difficulty</u>: 8. <u>Damage</u>: 20. <u>Reload time</u>: 10 mins. <u>Range</u>: 1 km. <u>Transportation</u>: 8/40. <u>Crew</u>: 4.<br /> - <i>Siege cannon</i>: <u>Difficulty</u>: 9. <u>Damage</u>: 25+. <u>Reload time</u>: 1 hour. <u>Range</u>: 750 metres. <u>Transportation</u>: 16/90. <u>Crew</u>: 6.<br /> <br /> - <a href=\"http://www.antiquesnavigator.com/ebay/images/2012/310370941267.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Siege Mortars</a>: Needing a smaller crew (one person can fire a mortar although it isn\u2019t easy - triple load time) and being easier to manoeuvre than large cannon, the mortar fires an explosive shell into the air. If it lands on target it can be devastating, but such pieces are difficult to aim and ammunition is scarce.<br /> <br /> <u>Difficulty</u>: 8. <u>Damage</u>: 20. <u>Reload time</u>: 30 mins. <u>Range</u>: 200 metres/1 km/2 km (see below). <u>Transportation</u>: 10/50. <u>Crew</u>: 1.<br /> <br /> <i>Mortars lob their shells and cannot hit nearby targets.</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3479,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","user":"von Lyttenburgh","date":". 02-22-2015, 01:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","name":"von Lyttenburgh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3479_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-19T18:41:31-08:00","id":412297,"title":"","content":"<b>Post Scriptum.</b><br /> <br /> Well, that's it. This is more or less all that I've made regarding the process of &quot;statting&quot; 17th century's arms and armour. It is by no means exhoustive and I understand it. E.g. I didn't provide stats for a full Chainmail, despite the fact that at that time it's a standard equipment for Polish <a href=\"https://s-media-cache-ak0.pinimg.com/736x/81/ca/2e/81ca2e31534ac668ce42e449aedee9aa.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Pancerni Cossacks</a>. There are already stats for chainmails in several sourcebooks. If you want to include them in your game, you already have everything you need.<br /> <br /> Most of all, I'd like to see some feedback, because I began working on all of this nearly 4 years ago. And although I tried to base my stats on the official mateiral when it was available and extrapolate when not, or to chose between two conflicting versions in different source books, I still think that some rules needs more polishing. I'm not a professional reconstructor and I don't even specialize in this time period as a historian. As I was translating and reposting Chapter about artillery I already felt that effective range rules might need some adjustment.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3479,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","user":"von Lyttenburgh","date":". 02-22-2015, 01:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","name":"von Lyttenburgh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3479_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"159"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}